The PROPER way to identify session hijacking in Wireshark
1️⃣ Filter attacker subnet
The challenge says the attacker is from:
Code

172.30.10.0/24
So filter:
Code

ip.addr == 172.30.10.0/24
This shows all attacker‑related packets.
2️⃣ Identify the TCP session being hijacked
Right‑click any attacker packet → Follow → TCP Stream
Inside the stream you will see two IPs:
	• One IP is 172.30.10.x → attacker
	• The other IP is the victim
In your capture, the two IPs are:
	• 172.30.10.200
	• 20.189.173.3
3️⃣ Look for hijacking indicators
Use this filter:
Code

tcp.analysis.flags
This highlights:
	• Retransmissions
	• Out‑of‑order packets
	• Duplicate ACKs
	• Spurious retransmissions
These appear because the attacker is injecting packets into the victim’s session.
Your screenshot already shows:
	• TCP Retransmission
	• ACK anomalies
This confirms hijacking.
4️⃣ Determine the victim
The challenge says:
	“Attacker tried to perform session hijacking on a machine from 172.30.10.0/24 subnet.”
So the victim is the machine inside that subnet.
Between the two IPs:
	• 172.30.10.200 → victim
	• 20.189.173.3 → remote server
This matches the challenge exactly.
